title: Genesis 13 – Toward Sodom
description: A verse-by-verse study of Genesis 13: Abram and Lot part ways, Lot pitches his tent toward Sodom, and God renews His promise to Abram and his seed, which is Christ.
output: lessons/genesis/genesis-13-toward-sodom.html

# Genesis series pattern (Genesis 1-5). Slide text is the author's own sentences from the Genesis 13 study
# (posts 6777-6780).

=== Introduction
--- Genesis Chapter 13 [title]
overline: Young Adult Sunday School • Genesis Series
heading: Genesis 13: Toward Sodom
sub: Back to the Altar • Lot’s Choice • The Promise Renewed
refs: Genesis 13:1-18
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

=== Back to the altar
--- Unto the Place of the Altar
kicker: vv. 1–4
sub: There Abram Called on the Name of the {Lord}
scripture: Genesis 13:1-4 | mark: Unto the place of the altar, which he had made there at the first
p: Chapter 13 of Genesis brings Abram and Sarai and his nephew Lot back to the land of Canaan, back to where Abram called upon the name of the {Lord}, near Bethel, which is on the west bank of the Jordan, in the area of Jerusalem and Bethlehem. There, scripture reminds us, he had built an altar. [[Genesis 12:8]]

--- Let There Be No Strife
kicker: vv. 5–9
sub: Between My Herdmen and Thy Herdmen
scripture: Genesis 13:5-9 | mark: Let there be no strife, I pray thee, between me and thee
p: Because of the greatness of his herds and Lot’s, and growing contentions between each other’s herdsmen, he gives Lot a choice of grazing land.

=== Lot’s choice
--- Lot Pitched His Tent Toward Sodom
kicker: vv. 10–13
sub: Even as the Garden of the {Lord}
scripture: Genesis 13:10-13 | mark: even as the garden of the LORD, like the land of Egypt; pitched his tent toward Sodom; wicked and sinners before the LORD exceedingly
p: Lot chooses the area around the plains of the Jordan, just above what’s called the Dead Sea today. Given the choice, and seeing there was much water and vegetation, *even as the garden of the {Lord}, like the land of Egypt*, and so enticed, he pitches his tent there to the east, toward Sodom.

--- We Pitch Our Tents Toward Sodom
kicker: Application • Genesis 13:12
sub: Choosing from Ease or Greed or Comfort
p: There’s many times in life we are given a choice like this, and choosing from ease or greed or comfort, we pitch our tents toward Sodom, toward wickedness and sin. Thinking about the times in my life where, rather than do what needed to be done to end strife, or to get through problems with myself or others, I instead pitched my tent toward Sodom. Trusting in a perceived gain over doing what was right. Choosing selfishly, rather than in grace. And often lured by the promise of worldly and carnal gains, ignoring the sin of those around me (and so ignoring my own), I did what Lot did when given a choice.
callout: Much like just Lot, who separated himself from righteous Abram and dwelled among the men of Sodom, throwing my lot (👀) in with men who were *wicked and sinners before the {Lord} exceedingly*. This temptation to choose the easy path leads to backsliding and often, to destruction. To years and talents and influence wasted that could have been in service to God and being a blessing to my people. [[2 Peter 2:7-8]]

=== The promise renewed
--- After That Lot Was Separated
kicker: vv. 14–17
sub: As the Dust of the Earth
scripture: Genesis 13:14-17 | mark: after that Lot was separated from him; to thee will I give it, and to thy seed for ever; as the dust of the earth
callout: God gives Abram two more promises, the land for ever and a seed too numerous to count. Pay attention to the order of events here. It is after Lot chooses to pitch his tent toward Sodom that God appears to Abram and gives him additional blessings, reinforcing His promise. This is how it usually works.

--- And to Thy Seed, Which Is Christ
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 13:15
fig: Galatians 3:16 | Galatians 3:16 | Christ is the answer to the promises, kept by God. Jesus is the seed through whom all nations are blessed.
fig: Galatians 3:29 | Galatians 3:29 | It is in Jesus that the promised land is given for ever, to those who believe and call upon the name of the {Lord}. It is in this seed that Abram can be said to have fathered a people too numerous to count, saved sons of God.

--- The Plain of Mamre
kicker: v. 18
sub: And Built There an Altar unto the {Lord}
scripture: Genesis 13:18 | mark: built there an altar unto the LORD
p: Abram, meanwhile, settles down to Hebron and the plains of Mamre. The Oak of Abraham, located in modern day Hebron, along the plains of Mamre, on the West Bank, is said to be thousands of years old. Traditions of men claim when it dies, it would signify the birth of the antichrist. It died in 1996.

--- Toward Sodom
center: Lot chose by sight and pitched his tent toward Sodom. Abram returned to the altar, and after Lot was separated from him, God renewed His promise. This is how it usually works.
